Images generated with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/image-playground/">Image Playground</a> and some photos edited with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a> will now include a SynthID watermark. Apple announced plans to add SynthID support back in June, and the feature is live in iOS 27.2.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/google-synthid.jpg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1069339" /><br/>
SynthID is a Google-created tool that identifies AI-generated content. It watermarks images and videos at the pixel level instead of adding metadata. Google says it does not affect image quality and is meant to survive cropping, filters, and compression. SynthID is used by Google, Apple, OpenAI, and others.<br/>
<br/>
There is no option on the iPhone for detecting SynthID watermarks, and to check for them, you need to use the <a href="https://synthid.com">SynthID Detector website</a>.<br/>
<br/>
When we tested the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/photos/">Photos</a> app tools, images altered with Extend and Reframe had SynthID watermarks when checked, but Clean Up watermarking seemed to depend on the size of the edit. With small edits, no watermark was added. Larger Clean Up edits included a SynthID watermark.<br/>
<br/>
